   "t": "Всё будет готово вовремя.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Again, and listen to how the cooks cheer him on.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ребята, давайте работать быстрее.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Guys, let's work faster.' We will focus on the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ребята",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a friendly term for a group. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "давайте",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is used with an action to mean 'let's'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "У нас ещё три блюда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We still have three dishes.' We can analyze the sentence: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "У",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "нас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is how you say 'we have'. After ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "три",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'three', the word for dish changes its ending.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Сейчас мы всё сделаем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We will do everything now.' Let's inspect the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "сделаем",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " describes a completed action, so it points to the future here. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Сейчас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " can mean 'right now' or 'in a second'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ну, чего вы стоите?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, why are you standing?' Let us analyze this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "чего",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means 'of what', and in casual speech it's a blunt 'why'. It is what you'd say to people who should be moving.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Шеф сказал работать быстрее.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'The chef said to work faster.' After ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "сказал",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", which means 'said', the order follows in its basic form. That's how you report an instruction.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Молодец, Лёва.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well done, Lyova.' We will analyze the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Молодец",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the standard way to say 'well done', and it can be used for anyone.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ты хороший шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You are a good chef.' The sentence has no verb, just 'you good chef'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "хороший",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " uses that ending because 'chef' refers to a man.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ты молодец.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You did great.' Lyova hands the praise straight back with ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ты",
